## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of low-level memory management, per-thread caching strategies, lock-free data structures, memory ordering semantics, and NUMA awareness in practical allocator design.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-size block allocators
- Thread-local storage (TLS) freelists with global slab pool
- Lock-free freelist with ABA avoidance using atomic compare-exchange
- Memory ordering: acquire/release semantics
- NUMA locality considerations and fragmentation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: alloc/free for fixed-size blocks
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; } Block;
Block* tls_freelist_head;
Block* global_pool_pull();

Block* alloc() {
  Block* b = tls_freelist_head;
  if (!b) {
    b = global_pool_pull();
    if (!b) return NULL; // OOM
  }
  tls_freelist_head = b->next;
  return b;
}

void free(Block* b) {
  b->next = tls_freelist_head;
  tls_freelist_head = b;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of a practical low-level concurrency primitive (SPSC) and memory ordering without locks in a real data-path scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Ring buffer wrap-around and capacity checks
- SPSC invariants: single producer advances tail, single consumer advances head
- Atomic head/tail with acquire/release semantics
- Correct visibility guarantees without heavy synchronization

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for SPSC queue
typedef struct { int data; } Item;
#define CAP 1024
Item buf[CAP];
std::atomic<size_t> head(0), tail(0);

bool enqueue(Item it){
  size_t h = head.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
  size_t t = tail.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
  if (t - h >= CAP) return false; // full
  buf[t % CAP] = it;
  tail.store(t+1, std::memory_order_release);
  return true;
}

bool dequeue(Item* out){
  size_t h = head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
  size_t t = tail.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
  if (t - h == 0) return false; // empty
  *out = buf[h % CAP];
  head.store(h+1, std::memory_order_release);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multiple producers/consumers without stalls?
- What tests would you add to detect drift or data loss under bursty input?

